WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.883 --> 00:00:03.112
A project changes how people work.

2
00:00:03.824 --> 00:00:08.561
So write the new way down as a short work instruction: one page per task.

3
00:00:09.591 --> 00:00:13.561
It lists the steps, in order, with photos of the real equipment.

4
00:00:14.629 --> 00:00:19.308
And for each step the key point: the detail that makes it go right or wrong.

5
00:00:20.056 --> 00:00:21.925
Write down why it matters, too.

6
00:00:23.248 --> 00:00:24.107
An example.

7
00:00:24.847 --> 00:00:29.073
A grid operator has its technicians report as-built data with an app:

8
00:00:29.212 --> 00:00:32.359
where the cable and its joints actually ended up.

9
00:00:33.056 --> 00:00:35.912
The joints are the connections between cable lengths.

10
00:00:36.952 --> 00:00:40.145
Write such an instruction with the people who do the work.

11
00:00:40.883 --> 00:00:45.690
Here you write it with the team leader: six steps, with photos of the app screens.

12
00:00:46.703 --> 00:00:50.999
One key point: photograph the joint before the trench is filled in again.

13
00:00:51.681 --> 00:00:52.052
Why?

14
00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:54.377
Because you can't afterwards.

15
00:00:55.458 --> 00:00:56.514
Then you test it.

16
00:00:57.268 --> 00:01:01.471
Let someone who wasn't involved follow the instruction while you watch.

17
00:01:02.201 --> 00:01:05.150
Where they hesitate, the instruction is unclear.

18
00:01:06.146 --> 00:01:10.058
At the grid operator, such a technician gets stuck on step 4.

19
00:01:10.789 --> 00:01:12.043
You rewrite that step.

20
00:01:13.370 --> 00:01:16.191
This way of instructing comes from TWI,

21
00:01:16.319 --> 00:01:22.043
Training Within Industry: a US wartime programme that started in 1940.

22
00:01:23.005 --> 00:01:28.404
By 1945 it had issued over 1.6 million certificates.

23
00:01:29.423 --> 00:01:32.186
Its Job Instruction method has four steps.

24
00:01:32.901 --> 00:01:34.166
One: prepare the learner.

25
00:01:34.884 --> 00:01:36.626
Say which task they will learn.

26
00:01:37.656 --> 00:01:42.788
Two: show and explain, one step at a time, and name the key point of each step.

27
00:01:43.831 --> 00:01:46.931
Three: let them try it themselves while you correct.

28
00:01:47.689 --> 00:01:50.092
Have them tell you the key points as they go.

29
00:01:51.077 --> 00:01:52.099
Four: follow up.

30
00:01:52.856 --> 00:01:57.755
They now work on their own, know whom to ask for help, and you check in regularly.

31
00:01:59.047 --> 00:02:02.948
The TWI motto still holds: if the worker hasn't learned,

32
00:02:03.134 --> 00:02:04.806
the instructor hasn't taught.

33
00:02:05.862 --> 00:02:08.602
Toyota adopted it in the nineteen fifties.

34
00:02:09.623 --> 00:02:10.691
Classroom or floor?

35
00:02:11.425 --> 00:02:16.231
A classroom is good for the why and the big picture, in 30 to 60 minutes.

36
00:02:17.264 --> 00:02:20.967
Skills are learned through on-the-job training: show it, do it together,

37
00:02:21.176 --> 00:02:24.114
let them do it while you watch, and check later.

38
00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:26.225
Who trains?

39
00:02:26.951 --> 00:02:30.782
Shift leads or experienced operators make the best trainers,

40
00:02:30.934 --> 00:02:32.327
if you train them first.

41
00:02:33.389 --> 00:02:35.537
And train just before people need it.

42
00:02:36.303 --> 00:02:40.320
A session six weeks before go-live is mostly forgotten by then.

43
00:02:41.325 --> 00:02:44.204
Plan short sessions in the week before go-live,

44
00:02:44.355 --> 00:02:47.258
and support on the floor in the first two weeks after.

45
00:02:48.282 --> 00:02:51.358
Cover every shift, including nights and weekends.

46
00:02:52.086 --> 00:02:54.036
Projects often forget those.

47
00:02:55.090 --> 00:03:00.616
At the grid operator, the technicians get 30 minutes in the morning meeting, on why.

48
00:03:01.366 --> 00:03:05.302
Then the team leader goes along on each technician's first job.

49
00:03:06.732 --> 00:03:08.148
Then check that it stuck.

50
00:03:08.918 --> 00:03:11.601
A week later, watch people do it without help.

51
00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:16.904
Not to judge them, but to see whether the instruction or the training needs fixing.

52
00:03:17.951 --> 00:03:22.652
When many people make the same mistake, the instruction is wrong, not the people.

53
00:03:23.653 --> 00:03:27.345
At the grid operator, a week later you check 20 reports.

54
00:03:28.044 --> 00:03:31.585
15 are complete, and 5 have no photo of the joint.

55
00:03:32.641 --> 00:03:37.436
All 5 come from crews who start at six, before the team leader arrives.

56
00:03:38.437 --> 00:03:41.049
So next week, a team leader starts early.

57
00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:44.390
Trainers often measure the wrong thing.

58
00:03:45.094 --> 00:03:51.352
The Kirkpatrick levels, from Donald Kirkpatrick in 1959, rank four checks.

59
00:03:52.042 --> 00:03:52.901
Did they like it?

60
00:03:53.653 --> 00:03:54.524
Did they learn it?

61
00:03:55.258 --> 00:03:56.524
Do they do it on the job?

62
00:03:57.282 --> 00:03:58.803
And did results improve?

63
00:03:59.875 --> 00:04:03.126
Most projects stop at the first: a smiley form.

64
00:04:04.175 --> 00:04:07.368
Experts argue whether a classroom is needed at all.

65
00:04:08.062 --> 00:04:12.649
You often hear the seventy, twenty, ten rule: seventy percent learned on the job,

66
00:04:12.776 --> 00:04:15.156
twenty from others, and ten in courses.

67
00:04:15.892 --> 00:04:18.574
It is a practitioner model with weak evidence.

68
00:04:20.005 --> 00:04:24.127
And if you don't know the task well yourself, let the expert train,

69
00:04:24.347 --> 00:04:26.054
and check the result yourself.
